// Test: bugfixes/func002_builtin_method_arguments_checked
// Grammar: method_call
// Semantic: FUNC002 / SEM001 — a built-in method (stdlib-reference.md §4)
//   takes its declared number and types of arguments.
// Fixed in: compiler 0.33.176
// Expected output:
// Expected error:
//   error[FUNC002]: 'string.substring' expects 2 argument(s), got 0
//   func002_builtin_method_arguments_checked.cln:21:
//   error[FUNC002]: 'string.toUpperCase' expects 0 argument(s), got 1
//   func002_builtin_method_arguments_checked.cln:22:
//   error[SEM001]: A value of type integer cannot be used as string
//   func002_builtin_method_arguments_checked.cln:23:
//
// Regression: built-in instance methods had no argument checks:
// `s.substring()` produced invalid WASM, extra arguments were dropped and
// `s.indexOf(5)` was accepted.

start:
	string s = "hello"
	string a = s.substring()
	string b = s.toUpperCase("x")
	integer c = s.indexOf(5)
	printl(a + b + c.toString())
